<td id="S4.T3.13.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.13.1.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">5.26</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.13.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.13.1.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">942.31M</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison with Different Geometry Priors.</span>
To further validate our design, we instantiate our framework with the DepthAnything backbone, following ISO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and EmbodiedOcc <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
Unlike ISO, which feeds depth into a heavy 3D U-Net, or EmbodiedOcc, which stacks a complex decoder on top of the backbone, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ours-DPT</span> attaches only a lightweight Gaussian head without any 3D convolutional decoder.
As shown in <a href="#S4.T3" title="In 4.4 Ablation studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Generalizing Visual Geometry Priors to Sparse Gaussian Occupancy Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, this variant already achieves stronger performance while being significantly more efficient: with the same depth prior, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ours-DPT</span> improves IoU from 53.55 to 56.96 and mIoU from 45.15 to 51.88 over EmbodiedOcc, while running nearly <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster (28.22 vs. 10.66 FPS) with less than half the parameters (97.95M vs. 231.45M).
<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ours-VGGT</span> further pushes accuracy to 63.14 IoU and 56.19 mIoU, while keeping the same lightweight occupancy head.
Taken together, these results show that our novel way of leveraging geometry priors yields both higher accuracy and substantially better efficiency, making fast and reliable occupancy prediction feasible for downstream embodied applications.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><span id="S4.T4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation on the number of sampled points <math id="S4.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> per ray.</span> Increasing <math id="S4.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> improves accuracy, but gains saturate beyond <math id="S4.T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}16</annotation></semantics></math> despite a sharp increase in the number of Gaussians.</figcaption>
