<td id="S4.T5.8.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T5.8.11.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">52.65</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.8.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T5.8.11.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">58.31</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.8.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T5.8.11.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">930</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of number of sampled points <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> along each ray.</span>
We study the impact of sampled number <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S4.T4" title="In 4.4 Ablation studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Generalizing Visual Geometry Priors to Sparse Gaussian Occupancy Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Increasing <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> consistently improves performance, as more samples enrich the coverage of volumetric interiors. However, the improvement saturates beyond <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=16</annotation></semantics></math>, where IoU and mIoU exhibit only marginal gains at <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=32</annotation></semantics></math> despite a substantial increase in the number of Gaussians.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of opacity threshold <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
We conduct ablation studies on the choice of opacity threshold <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> as shown in <a href="#S4.T5" title="In 4.4 Ablation studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Generalizing Visual Geometry Priors to Sparse Gaussian Occupancy Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. A smaller threshold (<em id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>) yields the best performance, achieving 56.19 mIoU and 63.14 IoU. As <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> increases, performance gradually degrades due to excessive pruning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Qualitative Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present qualitative comparisons on monocular occupancy prediction in <a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.3 Occupancy Prediction Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Generalizing Visual Geometry Priors to Sparse Gaussian Occupancy Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, where we compare against EmbodiedOcc <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. In addition, <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.3 Occupancy Prediction Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Generalizing Visual Geometry Priors to Sparse Gaussian Occupancy Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> illustrates two examples of our incremental update strategy on streaming inputs. More visualizations are provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We proposed GPOcc, a novel framework that leverages visual geometry priors for fine-grained occupancy prediction. By extending surface points along camera rays into volumetric samples, our method constructs sparse Gaussian primitives that compactly capture scene geometry. We further introduced an incremental update mechanism that adapts the framework seamlessly to streaming video inputs. Extensive experiments on Occ-ScanNet and EmbodiedOcc-ScanNet show that GPOcc achieves SoTA accuracy and generalizes across different priors, providing a scalable and effective solution for embodied 3D perception. Looking ahead, we believe GPOcc offers a promising step toward integrating strong geometric priors into broader embodied AI tasks, including interactive scene understanding, navigation and manipulation.</p>
</div>
</section>
